VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Simple vs complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what distinguishes a complex capital structure from a simple capital structure?', '["The entity has more than one class of common stock", "The entity has total assets exceeding $10 million", "The entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute EPS", "The entity has more than 1,000 shareholders of record"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities (such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments) that could potentially dilute basic EPS. A simple capital structure has only common stock outstanding with no potential dilution. Choice A is incorrect because multiple classes of common stock alone do not create a complex structure — potential dilution from convertible or contingent instruments is the defining factor.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearfield Inc. has 200,000 common shares outstanding and 50,000 shares of preferred stock that participates equally with common in dividends beyond its 6% cumulative preference. The controller needs to compute basic EPS for 2025. Under ASC 260, which method must Clearfield use to compute basic EPS?', '["The weighted-average method, using only common shares in the denominator", "The two-class method, allocating earnings between common and participating preferred", "The if-converted method, assuming conversion of preferred into common", "The treasury stock method, treating preferred shares as potential common shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, when an entity has participating securities that share in undistributed earnings with common stock, it must use the two-class method to compute basic EPS. This method allocates undistributed earnings between common shares and participating securities based on their respective participation rights. Choice A is incorrect because simply using weighted-average common shares ignores the participating preferred stock''s claim on undistributed earnings, which would overstate basic EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Effect of mid-year treasury stock repurchase on EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Quinlan Manufacturing had 500,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and repurchased 60,000 shares on September 1, 2025. Net income for 2025 was $1,760,000 with no preferred stock. Analyze how the treasury stock repurchase affects basic EPS compared to what EPS would have been without the repurchase.', '["EPS increases because the repurchase reduces weighted-average shares, increasing the per-share allocation of earnings", "EPS decreases because the cash used for the repurchase reduces net income", "EPS is unaffected because treasury stock transactions do not change weighted-average shares", "EPS decreases because the repurchase creates a dilutive effect similar to stock options"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-30-4, treasury stock repurchases reduce weighted-average common shares outstanding from the repurchase date forward. Quinlan''s weighted-average shares drop from 500,000 to 480,000 (500,000 x 8/12 + 440,000 x 4/12), increasing basic EPS from $3.52 to $3.67. Choice B is incorrect because the cash outflow for treasury stock is a balance sheet transaction that reduces equity, not a charge against net income on the income statement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Two-class vs if-converted for participating convertible preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashland Holdings has 300,000 common shares outstanding and 40,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 3 common shares. The preferred stock also participates equally in common dividends beyond its 8% preference. Net income is $960,000 and $160,000 in preferred dividends were declared. Evaluate which approach is most appropriate for computing diluted EPS under ASC 260.', '["Use only the if-converted method, adding back $160,000 and adding 120,000 shares", "Use only the two-class method, allocating undistributed earnings between common and preferred", "Use the treasury stock method, treating the preferred shares as options", "Use the more dilutive of the two-class or if-converted method for each potentially dilutive security"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, when a convertible security is also a participating security, the entity must use the more dilutive of the if-converted method or the two-class method. This dual test ensures that diluted EPS reflects the most conservative result. Choice A is incorrect because using only the if-converted method ignores the possibility that the two-class allocation may produce a more dilutive EPS, potentially overstating diluted earnings per share.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: hard / L3 — Retrospective EPS adjustment for post-period stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Systems reported basic EPS of $5.40 for the year ended December 31, 2025, based on net income of $2,700,000 and 500,000 weighted-average common shares. On February 15, 2026, before the financial statements were issued, Northgate declared a 3-for-1 stock split. Analyze the effect of this post-balance-sheet stock split on EPS as reported in the 2025 financial statements.', '["EPS is reported as $5.40 because the stock split occurred after year-end", "EPS is reported as $2.70 because only the second half of the year is adjusted", "EPS is retroactively adjusted to $1.80 to reflect the 3-for-1 split in all periods presented", "EPS is reported as $5.40 with only a footnote disclosure of the subsequent stock split"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be given retroactive effect in computing EPS for all periods presented. Northgate''s weighted-average shares are adjusted to 1,500,000 (500,000 x 3), yielding restated EPS of $1.80. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive adjustment for stock splits before issuance, not merely disclosure, to ensure comparability across periods.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Loss from continuing operations as control number
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitmore Industries reports a loss from continuing operations of $400,000 and a gain from discontinued operations of $600,000 (net of tax), resulting in net income of $200,000. Whitmore has 100,000 common shares and 10,000 dilutive stock options. Evaluate how the potentially dilutive securities should be treated when computing diluted EPS.', '["Include the options in diluted EPS for both continuing and discontinued operations because net income is positive", "Exclude the options from diluted EPS for all line items because the loss from continuing operations is the control number", "Include the options only for the discontinued operations line since that component shows income", "Include the options for net income only, excluding them from the individual components"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations is the control number for determining whether securities are dilutive. Because Whitmore reports a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive and must be excluded from diluted EPS for all line items, including discontinued operations. Choice A is incorrect because positive net income does not override the continuing operations control number under ASC 260.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — RSUs and diluted EPS using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mapleridge Corp. has 150,000 common shares outstanding and 12,000 unvested restricted stock units (RSUs) with no exercise price. The average unrecognized compensation cost is $18 per RSU, and the average market price of Mapleridge''s stock during 2025 was $60 per share. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added for diluted EPS?', '["8,400 shares", "12,000 shares", "3,600 shares", "6,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method, where assumed proceeds equal the remaining unrecognized compensation cost ($18 x 12,000 = $216,000). These proceeds hypothetically repurchase 3,600 shares ($216,000 / $60), yielding 8,400 incremental shares (12,000 - 3,600). Choice B is incorrect because it includes all RSU shares without accounting for the assumed repurchase from unamortized compensation cost, overstating dilution.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under U.S. GAAP", "All entities with revenues exceeding $50 million annually", "All entities with more than 500 shareholders of record", "Public entities with common stock or potential common stock traded in a public market"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public business entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market, including stock exchanges and over-the-counter markets. Nonpublic entities are exempt from this requirement. Choice A is incorrect because private companies preparing GAAP financial statements are not required to present EPS, though they may voluntarily do so.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Shares issued for acquisition weighted-average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Veridian Corp. had 400,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On August 1, 2025, Veridian issued 90,000 shares to acquire Creston Inc. in a business combination. What is Veridian''s weighted-average number of common shares for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["490,000", "445,000", "437,500", "400,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-30-1, shares issued in a business combination are included in weighted-average shares from the acquisition date. Veridian''s weighted-average is 400,000 x 7/12 + 490,000 x 5/12 = 437,500 shares. Choice A is incorrect because using 490,000 assumes the acquisition shares were outstanding for the entire year, which overstates the denominator and understates EPS for pre-acquisition periods.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does it mean for a security to be classified as antidilutive?', '["The security reduces basic EPS when excluded from the calculation", "Including the security in diluted EPS would increase EPS or decrease loss per share", "The security has an exercise price equal to the current market price", "The security was issued during the current reporting period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, an antidilutive security is one whose inclusion in the diluted EPS computation would increase earnings per share or decrease loss per share compared to basic EPS. Antidilutive securities must be excluded from diluted EPS. Choice C is incorrect because options with an exercise price equal to the market price produce zero incremental shares under the treasury stock method and are simply neutral, not specifically antidilutive.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Discontinued operations EPS presentation location
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, where must earnings per share for discontinued operations be presented?', '["On the face of the income statement or in the notes to the financial statements", "Only in the notes to the financial statements", "Only on the face of the income statement, never in the notes", "In the management discussion and analysis section only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, an entity must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. EPS for discontinued operations may be presented either on the face of the income statement or in the notes to the financial statements. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically permits disclosure of discontinued operations EPS in the notes rather than requiring it on the income statement face.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Convertible debt premium effect on diluted EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonecrest Corp. issued $2,000,000 face value of 7% convertible bonds at 105, convertible into 50,000 common shares. The premium is amortized over 10 years using the straight-line method. The tax rate is 25%. Analyze how the bond premium amortization affects the numerator adjustment when computing diluted EPS in the first year.', '["$105,000 because the premium does not affect the interest add-back", "$140,000 because the full coupon is added back before tax", "$130,000 because premium amortization is excluded from the numerator", "$97,500 because premium amortization reduces interest expense, lowering the after-tax add-back"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back the after-tax interest expense eliminated upon conversion. Premium amortization reduces interest expense from $140,000 to $130,000 ($140,000 - $10,000 annual amortization), so the after-tax add-back is $97,500 ($130,000 x 0.75). Choice A is incorrect because it ignores premium amortization and uses $140,000 x 0.75 = $105,000, overstating the numerator adjustment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Anti-dilutive test applied individually not in aggregate
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Parkfield Corp. has basic EPS of $4.00. It has two convertible securities: Security A would add $60,000 to the numerator and 10,000 shares (per-share impact $6.00), and Security B would add $20,000 and 8,000 shares (per-share impact $2.50). If both were included simultaneously, diluted EPS would be $4.22. Analyze which securities should be included in diluted EPS.', '["Include both securities because their combined effect of $4.22 is close to basic EPS", "Exclude both securities because the aggregate per-share impact exceeds basic EPS", "Include only Security B because each security must be tested individually for dilutive effect", "Include only Security A because it has the larger per-share impact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, each potentially dilutive security must be tested individually by ranking from most dilutive to least dilutive. Security B''s per-share impact of $2.50 is below basic EPS of $4.00 (dilutive), while Security A''s $6.00 exceeds basic EPS (antidilutive). Only Security B is included. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 prohibits aggregating antidilutive securities with dilutive ones — each security must pass the dilution test independently before inclusion.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summerfield Corp. has written put options obligating it to repurchase 20,000 shares at $50 per share. The average market price during 2025 was $40 per share. Summerfield has 300,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $1,200,000. Evaluate the effect of these written put options on diluted EPS.', '["The puts are antidilutive because the exercise price exceeds the market price", "The puts add 5,000 incremental shares to the denominator because the exercise price exceeds the market price, creating dilution", "The puts reduce diluted EPS by $0.20 because 20,000 shares are assumed repurchased at a premium", "The puts have no effect on diluted EPS because put options are always excluded under ASC 260"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options use the reverse treasury stock method. The entity assumes it issues 25,000 shares at the $40 average market price to raise $1,000,000, then repurchases 20,000 shares at the $50 exercise price, yielding 5,000 net incremental shares. This is dilutive when the exercise price exceeds market price. Choice A is incorrect because for written puts (unlike call options), a higher exercise price means the entity overpays, creating dilution rather than antidilution.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Actual mid-year bond conversion diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeline Corp. had $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds outstanding on January 1, 2025. On July 1, 2025, all bondholders converted into 25,000 common shares. Ridgeline had 100,000 common shares before conversion and net income of $500,000. The tax rate is 25%. What is diluted EPS?', '["$4.18", "$4.44", "$4.00", "$4.36"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when bonds convert during the period, diluted EPS assumes conversion at the beginning of the year. The numerator adds back after-tax interest for the pre-conversion period ($30,000 x 0.75 = $22,500), and the denominator includes 25,000 conversion shares for the full year (125,000 total), yielding $522,500 / 125,000 = $4.18. Choice C ($4.00) is incorrect because it omits the interest add-back, dividing $500,000 by 125,000 and double-penalizing the numerator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Definition of treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the purpose of the treasury stock method in computing diluted EPS?', '["Calculating the effect of treasury stock repurchases on weighted-average shares", "Determining the gain or loss on reissuance of treasury shares", "Measuring the fair value of stock options for compensation expense", "Determining the dilutive effect of options and warrants by assuming exercise proceeds repurchase shares at the average market price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes that proceeds from the exercise of stock options and warrants are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price during the period. The difference between shares assumed issued and shares assumed repurchased represents the incremental dilutive shares. Choice A is incorrect because actual treasury stock repurchases affect weighted-average shares directly — the treasury stock method specifically addresses hypothetical dilution from options and warrants.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — ESPP incremental shares for diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarbrook Inc. has a compensatory employee stock purchase plan (ESPP) under which employees can purchase up to 10,000 shares at $30 per share (85% of the grant-date market price). The average market price of Cedarbrook''s stock during 2025 was $50 per share. Using the treasury stock method under ASC 260, how many incremental shares are included in diluted EPS?', '["10,000 shares", "6,000 shares", "4,000 shares", "1,500 shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, compensatory ESPPs are treated like options under the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds are 10,000 x $30 = $300,000, which hypothetically repurchases 6,000 shares ($300,000 / $50). The incremental shares are 10,000 - 6,000 = 4,000. Choice A is incorrect because it includes all ESPP shares without netting out the shares assumed repurchased with exercise proceeds, overstating the dilutive effect by 6,000 shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Cumulative vs noncumulative preferred dividend deduction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which type of preferred dividends must be deducted from net income when computing basic earnings per share?', '["Only dividends that were declared and paid in cash during the period", "Declared dividends for noncumulative preferred and the annual dividend for cumulative preferred regardless of declaration", "Only dividends declared by the board, with no adjustment for cumulative undeclared dividends", "All preferred dividends, whether cumulative or noncumulative, regardless of declaration"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, basic EPS deducts preferred dividends from net income. For noncumulative preferred, only declared dividends are deducted. For cumulative preferred, the annual dividend amount is deducted regardless of whether declared because the cumulative right creates a prior claim on earnings. Choice C is incorrect because it ignores undeclared cumulative preferred dividends, which must always be deducted to prevent overstating earnings available to common shareholders.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Contracts settlable in stock or cash presumption
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when a contract can be settled in either common stock or cash at the entity''s option, what is the presumption for diluted EPS purposes?', '["The contract will be settled in common stock if the effect is dilutive", "The contract will be settled in cash because that is the most conservative assumption", "The entity may choose whichever settlement method produces higher EPS", "The contract is excluded from diluted EPS until settlement actually occurs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-45, when a contract may be settled in stock or cash, the entity must presume share settlement for diluted EPS purposes if the effect is dilutive. This ensures diluted EPS reflects the maximum potential dilution from outstanding contracts. Choice B is incorrect because presuming cash settlement would understate potential dilution, contradicting ASC 260''s objective of presenting the most dilutive scenario for existing common shareholders.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Unrecognized compensation cost in assumed proceeds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brightwell Corp. granted 15,000 stock options with an exercise price of $25 on January 1, 2025. Total compensation cost is $180,000, vesting over 3 years. In Year 1, $60,000 was recognized and the average market price was $50. Analyze how the $120,000 of unrecognized compensation cost affects the number of incremental shares in diluted EPS compared to using only the exercise price as assumed proceeds.', '["The unrecognized cost has no effect because only the exercise price determines assumed proceeds", "The unrecognized cost increases incremental shares from 5,100 to 7,500", "The unrecognized cost eliminates all incremental shares, making the options antidilutive", "The unrecognized cost reduces incremental shares from 7,500 to 5,100 because it increases assumed proceeds"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds for the treasury stock method include the exercise price plus average unrecognized compensation cost. Including $120,000 raises assumed proceeds from $375,000 to $495,000, repurchasing 9,900 shares instead of 7,500, reducing incremental shares from 7,500 to 5,100. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly requires including unrecognized compensation cost as part of assumed proceeds, reflecting the economic cost not yet expensed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Quarterly vs year-to-date dilution testing
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinebrook Corp. has 10,000 stock options with an exercise price of $30. The average market price was $40 in Q1 and $25 in Q2. For the six-month year-to-date period, the average market price was $32.50. Analyze whether the options are included in diluted EPS for Q1, Q2, and the year-to-date period.', '["Dilutive in Q1 and year-to-date, but antidilutive and excluded in Q2", "Dilutive in all three periods because the exercise price is below the overall average", "Antidilutive in all three periods because the Q2 result eliminates prior dilution", "Dilutive in Q1 only, with the year-to-date period following the Q2 treatment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, dilution is tested independently for each reporting period using that period''s average market price. In Q1 ($40 > $30), options are dilutive. In Q2 ($25 < $30), they are antidilutive and excluded. For the year-to-date period, the six-month average of $32.50 exceeds $30, so options are dilutive. Choice B is incorrect because each interim period uses its own average market price, and Q2''s lower average makes options antidilutive for that quarter regardless of other periods.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred shares and EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Gardenview Corp. issued 10,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock at $100 per share with a 5% annual dividend. Under ASC 480, these shares are classified as liabilities. The company reports net income of $800,000 and has 200,000 common shares outstanding. Evaluate how the mandatorily redeemable preferred stock affects the computation of basic EPS.', '["$3.75 because $50,000 in preferred dividends must be deducted from net income", "$3.50 because both the dividends and accretion to redemption value are deducted", "$4.00 because the preferred dividends are already reflected as interest expense in net income", "$4.25 because the preferred dividends should be added back as a non-cash charge"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 480-10-25, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, and its dividends are recorded as interest expense rather than equity distributions. Because the $50,000 is already deducted as an expense in arriving at net income of $800,000, no further adjustment to the EPS numerator is needed, yielding $4.00. Choice A is incorrect because deducting preferred dividends again would double-count the charge, treating a liability-classified instrument as if it were equity.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Nonpublic entity EPS exemption
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which statement is true about earnings per share disclosure for nonpublic entities?', '["Nonpublic entities must present basic EPS but may omit diluted EPS", "Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS but may voluntarily do so", "Nonpublic entities must present EPS only if they have outstanding stock options", "Nonpublic entities must present EPS if total revenues exceed $25 million"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, the EPS disclosure requirements apply only to public business entities with common stock or potential common stock traded in public markets. Nonpublic entities have no obligation to present EPS data, though they may voluntarily disclose it following the same guidance. Choice A is incorrect because nonpublic entities are fully exempt from both basic and diluted EPS presentation — there is no partial requirement to present only basic EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Rights offering bonus element adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxworth Corp. has 200,000 common shares outstanding and announces a rights offering allowing shareholders to buy one new share for every four shares held at $20 per share. The market price before the offering is $40 and the theoretical ex-rights price is $36. Under ASC 260, what adjustment is required to weighted-average shares for periods before the rights offering?', '["No adjustment because rights offerings are not retroactive events", "Multiply by 0.90 to reflect the 10% dilution from new shares", "Multiply by 1.25 to account for the 1-for-4 issuance ratio", "Multiply by the ratio of the pre-rights market price to the theoretical ex-rights price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-17, when a rights offering includes a bonus element (subscription price below market), weighted-average shares for periods before the offering are adjusted by the ratio of the fair value per share before exercise to the theoretical ex-rights price ($40 / $36 = 1.111). This ensures comparability across periods. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically requires retroactive adjustment for the bonus element in rights offerings, similar to stock dividends.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Performance-based RSUs with unmet conditions
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Wyndham Solutions granted 30,000 performance-based restricted stock units on January 1, 2025, that vest only if cumulative revenue exceeds $50 million over three years. At December 31, 2025, cumulative revenue is $14 million and management does not expect to meet the $50 million target. The average unrecognized compensation cost per RSU is $12 and the average market price is $45. Evaluate how these RSUs should be treated in the 2025 diluted EPS calculation.', '["Exclude the RSUs from diluted EPS because the performance condition is not satisfied at year-end", "Include all 30,000 RSUs using the treasury stock method because they represent potential shares", "Include 8,400 RSUs proportionally based on the 28% revenue progress toward the target", "Include the RSUs only if their inclusion would increase diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares (including performance-based RSUs) are included in diluted EPS only if the performance conditions would be satisfied as of the reporting date. Because Wyndham''s cumulative revenue of $14 million does not meet the $50 million threshold at December 31, 2025, the RSUs are excluded. Choice B is incorrect because performance-based RSUs require the condition to be currently met — merely being outstanding as potential shares is insufficient for inclusion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Forward purchase contract and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. entered a forward contract on March 1, 2025, obligating it to repurchase 15,000 of its own shares at $55 per share on December 31, 2025. The average market price during 2025 was $44. Meridian has 250,000 common shares and net income of $1,000,000. Analyze the effect of this forward purchase contract on diluted EPS.', '["The contract has no effect because the shares have not yet been repurchased", "The contract adds 3,750 incremental shares because the repurchase price exceeds the market price", "The contract reduces shares by 15,000 because forward repurchases are treated as immediate buybacks", "The contract adds 15,000 incremental shares to reflect the full repurchase obligation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, forward purchase contracts use the reverse treasury stock method when the repurchase price exceeds the average market price. Meridian assumes issuing 18,750 shares at $44 to raise $825,000, then repurchasing 15,000 shares at $55, yielding 3,750 net incremental shares. Choice C is incorrect because forward contracts are not treated as completed transactions — ASC 260 uses a hypothetical settlement approach that captures only the dilutive premium above market price.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Stock dividend retroactive weighted-average adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Belmont Industries has 300,000 common shares outstanding for the entire year. On June 1, 2025, Belmont declares and distributes a 5% common stock dividend. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for computing basic EPS for 2025?', '["306,250", "300,000", "315,000", "308,750"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are given retroactive effect to the beginning of the earliest period presented when computing weighted-average shares. The 5% dividend adds 15,000 shares (300,000 x 5%), making the weighted-average 315,000 for the entire year. Choice D (308,750) is incorrect because it treats the stock dividend as a new issuance from June 1, weighting the additional shares for only 7 months instead of retroactively applying them to the full year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Warrants incremental shares calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornberry Corp. has 20,000 warrants outstanding, each exercisable for one common share at $30 per warrant. The average market price during 2025 was $50 per share. Using the treasury stock method under ASC 260, how many incremental shares are included in diluted EPS?', '["20,000 shares", "12,000 shares", "10,000 shares", "8,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes 20,000 warrants are exercised at $30, generating $600,000 in proceeds. These proceeds hypothetically repurchase 12,000 shares at the $50 average market price ($600,000 / $50). The incremental shares are 20,000 - 12,000 = 8,000. Choice B (12,000) is incorrect because it represents the shares assumed repurchased with exercise proceeds, not the net incremental dilutive shares that increase the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Ranking multiple convertibles by per-share impact
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summit Financial has basic EPS of $5.00 with net income of $1,500,000 and 300,000 shares. Three convertible securities exist: Security X (per-share impact $3.00), Security Y (per-share impact $5.50), and Security Z (per-share impact $4.20). Analyze the correct order for testing these securities when computing diluted EPS.', '["X first, then Z; Y is excluded as antidilutive because securities are ranked from most to least dilutive", "Y first, then Z, then X, testing from highest to lowest per-share impact", "All three are tested simultaneously in a single computation", "X first, then Y, then Z, testing in alphabetical order"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities are ranked from most dilutive (lowest per-share impact) to least dilutive before sequential testing. Security X ($3.00) is tested first, then Z ($4.20). Security Y ($5.50) exceeds basic EPS and is antidilutive, so it is excluded. This ranking ensures each inclusion is tested against the running diluted EPS. Choice B is incorrect because testing from highest to lowest impact would incorrectly process antidilutive Security Y first, distorting the computation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted numerator add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Corp. has net income of $700,000, 100,000 common shares, and 5,000 shares of $100 par, 7% convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 5 common shares. Preferred dividends of $35,000 were declared. Basic EPS is $6.65. What is diluted EPS under the if-converted method?', '["$6.65", "$5.60", "$5.32", "$7.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back the $35,000 preferred dividends to the numerator (yielding $700,000) and adds 25,000 conversion shares to the denominator (yielding 125,000 shares), producing diluted EPS of $5.60 ($700,000 / 125,000). Choice C ($5.32) is incorrect because it fails to add back preferred dividends when assuming conversion, dividing $665,000 by 125,000 and double-penalizing the numerator.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
